WEBVTT

00:00:01.710 --> 00:00:02.799
Hi, I'm Orrery!

00:00:02.799 --> 00:00:06.792
Here is how DeepInfer, DeepSeek's newly opened engine, serves one request.

00:00:09.058 --> 00:00:10.642
The release is the engine.

00:00:10.642 --> 00:00:14.443
The storage, the transfer and the balancers are described, but kept back.

00:00:18.342 --> 00:00:21.743
A request enters through the recipe, which makes tokens, and the

00:00:21.743 --> 00:00:22.980
entrypoint, which routes it.

00:00:23.400 --> 00:00:27.601
Prefill and decode are separate services, each behind its own balancer, over

00:00:27.601 --> 00:00:29.001
a shared cache store.

00:00:30.675 --> 00:00:32.511
The recipe encodes the request.

00:00:32.511 --> 00:00:35.449
The entrypoint matches its prefix against the store.

00:00:35.869 --> 00:00:40.153
A prefill engine swaps the cached part in, and computes only the new tokens.

00:00:40.573 --> 00:00:44.176
The cache and window state move to a decode engine on sixty

00:00:44.176 --> 00:00:45.377
four GPUs or more.

00:00:45.797 --> 00:00:48.506
Decode streams tokens out, and writes its cache back.

00:00:50.458 --> 00:00:53.010
Many layers look back over a short sliding window.

00:00:53.430 --> 00:00:58.296
After a cache hit, DeepInfer recomputes just the last hundred and twenty eight tokens.

00:00:58.296 --> 00:00:59.338
It is approximate.

00:01:01.067 --> 00:01:02.061
Decode uses DSpark.

00:01:02.061 --> 00:01:04.713
The trick is choosing how much to verify.

00:01:05.133 --> 00:01:06.951
Each request drafts five tokens.

00:01:08.064 --> 00:01:10.766
A confidence head rates each draft's chance to survive.

00:01:11.186 --> 00:01:13.593
Every draft in the batch is sorted by that chance.

00:01:14.339 --> 00:01:17.934
The scheduler adds them until tokens per millisecond stop rising.

00:01:19.275 --> 00:01:20.399
Here is the formula.

00:01:21.275 --> 00:01:24.193
The headline chart measures steps per second, times tau.

00:01:24.613 --> 00:01:28.161
Tau assumes three drafts, each accepted three quarters of the time.

00:01:28.581 --> 00:01:31.728
DeepSeek's own numbers suggest that is high by about a sixth.

00:01:33.092 --> 00:01:37.112
On one denominator, at matched speed, DeepInfer's chart is about one and

00:01:37.112 --> 00:01:39.457
a half times the best vLLM run.

00:01:40.642 --> 00:01:41.925
The engine is open.

00:01:41.925 --> 00:01:45.453
The service around it, and a like-for-like benchmark, are not yet.

00:01:47.692 --> 00:01:51.217
An open engine, a greedy verifier, and a chart to read closely.

00:01:52.292 --> 00:01:54.375
Every source is in the full article.

00:01:54.375 --> 00:01:54.970
I'm Orrery.

00:01:54.970 --> 00:01:55.268
Bye!
